    <name>
      <text>To find y′ for the equation  implicit differentiation</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find \(y'\) for \(\displaystyle {@eq@}\)</p>
<p>\(y'=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To find \(y'\) for the equation \(\displaystyle {@eq@}\), we can use implicit differentiation.</p>

<p>Differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to \(x\):</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\left({@fx@}\right) + \frac{d}{dx}\left({@fy@}\right) = \frac{d}{dx}\left({@const@}\right)\)</p>
 
<p>Now, apply the chain rule to the \(\displaystyle{@fy@}\) term:</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle
{@dfx@} + {@dfy@}  \frac{dy}{dx} = {@dfc@} 
\)</p>

<p>Now, solve for \(\displaystyle\frac{dy}{dx}:\)</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle
{@dfy@}  \frac{dy}{dx} = -{@dfx@} 
\)</p>

<p>Divide both sides by \(\displaystyle{@dfy@}\):</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle
\frac{dy}{dx} = -\frac{{@dfx@}}{{@dfy@}}= {@ta@}
\)</p>

<p>So, the derivative of \(y\) with respect to \(x\) is \( \displaystyle \frac{dy}{dx} = {@ta@}\), that is, \( \displaystyle y' = {@ta@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
